# =============================================================================
# HarryAfters — copy this file to `.env` and fill in values (never commit `.env`).
# Same variables go in Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables.
# After editing: restart `npm run dev`. Verify locally:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/debug/register-env
#   http://localhost:3000/api/debug/register-test
# =============================================================================
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deploying to Vercel (Stripe still in Test / “sandbox” mode)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Import the repo in Vercel (or run `npx vercel` from the project root).
#    Build is `nuxt build` — `nuxt.config.ts` already sets nitro.preset = vercel.
#
# 2. In Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables, add every NUXT_* below.
#    Use “Production” (and Preview if you want PRs to work the same).
#
# 3. Stripe MUST stay in Test mode while you use sk_test_ keys:
#    Dashboard → toggle “Test mode” ON (top right).
#    - Developers → API keys → copy Secret key (sk_test_...) → NUXT_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
#    - Developers → Webhooks → Add endpoint
#        URL: https://<your-deployment>.vercel.app/api/stripe/webhook
#        Event: checkout.session.completed
#      Open the new endpoint → “Reveal” signing secret (whsec_...) →
#      NUXT_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#    Test and Live webhooks use different whsec_ values — never mix sk_test_ with
#    a Live webhook secret or vice versa.
#
# 4. NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL — set your canonical URL, e.g.
#        https://your-project.vercel.app
#    If you omit it on Vercel, the app falls back to https://$VERCEL_URL (fine
#    for previews; for a custom domain, set this explicitly so emails/links match).
#
# 5. After deploy: run a test checkout with card 4242 4242 4242 4242. Confirm
#    the webhook in Stripe → Developers → Webhooks → your endpoint → “Recent deliveries”.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase (database for registrations)
# Dashboard: https://supabase.com/dashboard → your project
# Docs: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/api/api-keys
#
# NUXT_SUPABASE_URL
#   (Not on the same screen as only “Publishable / Secret” keys — the URL is separate.)
#   Easiest: project sidebar → “Connect” (or “Connect to your project”) — copy “Project URL”.
#   Or: Project Settings → Data API (sometimes “Integrations” → Data API) — API URL / Project URL.
#   Shape: https://<short-ref>.supabase.co  (no path; not the /rest/v1 URL)
#
# NUXT_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY  (server only — never expose to the browser)
#   Preferred: Settings → API Keys → Secret key → copy a key starting with sb_secret_
#   Legacy:   same page → “Legacy anon / service_role” → copy service_role (long JWT)
#   Do NOT use: publishable / anon / sb_publishable_ (those are for client apps)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_SUPABASE_URL=
NUXT_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resend (optional mirror of signups into a mailing list / audience)
# Dashboard: https://resend.com
#
# NUXT_RESEND_API_KEY
#   Resend → API Keys → Create / copy key (usually starts with re_)
#
# NUXT_RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID
#   Resend → Audiences (or Segments, depending on UI) → open your list → copy its ID
#   Often looks like a UUID or aud_ / segment-style id from the URL or detail panel
#
# NUXT_RESEND_FROM  (required for the signup confirmation email)
#   Format: "Display Name <address@domain.com>"  or  address@domain.com
#   Testing without your own domain: use onboarding@resend.dev (Resend → Domains docs)
#   Production: verify your domain in Resend, then e.g. hello@mail.yourdomain.com
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_RESEND_API_KEY=
NUXT_RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=
NUXT_RESEND_FROM=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin token — you invent this value (any long random string)
# Used by:
#   GET  /api/admin/registrations   header: x-admin-token
#   POST /api/admin/announce        header: x-admin-token  (fires the broadcast)
#   POST /api/admin/check-in        header: x-admin-token  (door scan)
# Generate e.g. openssl rand -hex 32   or   Node: require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex')
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe (ticket payments)
# Dashboard: https://dashboard.stripe.com  -> Developers -> API keys
#
# NUXT_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
#   Test mode: starts with sk_test_   |   Live mode: starts with sk_live_
#
# NUXT_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#   Local dev: run `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook`
#              and paste the printed `whsec_...` value here.
#   Production: Stripe Dashboard -> Developers -> Webhooks -> Add endpoint
#              URL: https://<your-site>/api/stripe/webhook
#              Event:    checkout.session.completed
#              Then copy the "Signing secret" (whsec_...).
#
# NUXT_CHECKOUT_TOTAL_PENCE  (recommended — full amount charged, in pence)
#   £6.30 total = 630. Entry line on Stripe = this minus booking fee (630 − 30 = £6.00 entry).
#
# NUXT_TICKET_PRICE_PENCE  (legacy — entry ticket only, excludes booking fee)
#   Only if you omit NUXT_CHECKOUT_TOTAL_PENCE. Example: £6.00 entry = 600.
#   If you mistakenly set 630 here with a 30p fee, the app treats it as £6.30 total (not £6.60);
#   prefer NUXT_CHECKOUT_TOTAL_PENCE=630 and leave this empty.
#
# NUXT_BOOKING_FEE_PENCE
#   Separate line on Stripe Checkout. Default 30 (£0.30).
#
# If you omit both total and ticket, defaults apply: £6.30 total, 30p fee, £6.00 entry.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
NUXT_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
NUXT_CHECKOUT_TOTAL_PENCE=630
NUXT_TICKET_PRICE_PENCE=
NUXT_BOOKING_FEE_PENCE=30

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public site URL (used for Stripe success/cancel URLs + ticket links).
# Local: http://localhost:3000
# Prod:  https://your-domain.com
#        Use HTTPS in prod; verify the domain under Stripe → Settings →
#        Payment methods → Apple Pay so Apple Pay works on the hosted checkout.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Homepage mode — flip when ticket sales go live
#
# NUXT_TICKETS_LIVE
#   Server-only. When true, `/` shows “tickets on sale” (hero + CTAs to /buy),
#   hides the registration form & interest popup, and updates the footer CTA.
#   Accepts: true / false / 1 / 0 / yes / no (case-insensitive).
#   After changing on Vercel: redeploy so Nitro picks up the new env value.
#
#   Sanity check: GET /api/site-mode  →  { "ticketsLive": true }
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_TICKETS_LIVE=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live Jukebox (Spotify-powered song queue)
#
# Architecture in three pieces:
#   1. Vercel app — search proxy + queue API + admin OAuth bootstrap.
#   2. Supabase  — canonical queue (song_queue, song_votes, now_playing,
#                  spotify_account). Run supabase/schema.sql to provision.
#   3. Home server — long-lived worker (`scripts/jukebox-worker.mjs`) that polls
#                    Supabase, calls Spotify Player API on the host's Premium
#                    account, and writes back the now_playing snapshot.
#
# Set up the Spotify app:
#   - https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard → Create app
#   - Add redirect URI:  ${NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL}/api/admin/jukebox/spotify/callback
#     (Local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/admin/jukebox/spotify/callback)
#   - Copy Client ID + Secret below.
#
# NUXT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID / NUXT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET
#   Used for both the public client_credentials token (search) and the user
#   OAuth bootstrap.
#
# NUXT_SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI
#   Must exactly match a Redirect URI registered on the Spotify app, e.g.
#   https://harryafters.example.com/api/admin/jukebox/spotify/callback
#
# One-time bootstrap (run from the Premium account's browser):
#   1. Visit  ${NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL}/api/admin/jukebox/spotify/login?token=<NUXT_ADMIN_TOKEN>
#   2. Approve the scopes (user-modify-playback-state, user-read-playback-state,
#      user-read-currently-playing).
#   3. You'll see "Jukebox linked" — the refresh token is now in
#      public.spotify_account on Supabase.
#
# Run the worker on your home server:
#   - Node 20+ required (uses --env-file).
#   - `npm install` once (uses @supabase/supabase-js already in deps).
#   - `npm run jukebox:worker` (resolves to
#      `node --env-file=.env scripts/jukebox-worker.mjs`).
#   - Keep it alive via pm2 (`pm2 start npm --name jukebox -- run jukebox:worker`),
#     systemd unit, or Windows Task Scheduler.
#   - The worker only needs NUXT_SUPABASE_URL, NUXT_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY,
#     NUXT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID, NUXT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET. It NEVER takes
#     incoming traffic — it only dials out to Supabase + Spotify.
#
# Premium-only: Spotify Web API playback control requires a Premium account.
# An "active device" (any device that recently played) is also required, so
# press play on Spotify once before the night so the worker has somewhere to
# enqueue.
#
# Optional worker tuning (Proxmox/home `.env`):
#   JUKEBOX_POLL_MS / JUKEBOX_LOOKAHEAD_MS — poll interval & enqueue window.
#   Leave JUKEBOX_ALLOW_SPOTIFY_REPEAT unset so the worker turns Spotify repeat off
#   periodically (repeat-one blocks the queue). Set to 1 only if you insist on repeat.
#   JUKEBOX_REPEAT_OFF_INTERVAL_MS (default 40000), JUKEBOX_STALE_ENQUEUE_MS (18000),
#   JUKEBOX_HANDOFF_BUFFER_MS (12000) — recovery when Spotify/user skips confuse state.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
NUXT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
NUXT_SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/admin/jukebox/spotify/callback
